WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.031
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.031 --> 00:00:10.423
I'm Jonathan Burke,
the AP Germany professor of finance at

00:00:10.423 --> 00:00:14.450
the Graduate School of Business
at Stanford University.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:17.531
&gt;&gt; And I'm Jules Van Vince Bergen,
the Nippon life professor in finance at

00:00:17.531 --> 00:00:20.100
the Wharton School at
the University of Pennsylvania.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.540
Join us on a journey as we
explore the science and

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.750
strategy of better decision making.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.650
&gt;&gt; Today, we will consider
an important business decision.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:30.354
The decision about whether
to buy a good business.

00:00:30.354 --> 00:00:35.203
Whether you're a sole proprietor, think
of buying an established small business,

00:00:35.203 --> 00:00:39.040
well, conglomerate concerning
the decision to buy up a new brand or

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.280
starting a new division,
the same question needs to be answered.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.929
What is a good business to buy?

00:00:45.929 --> 00:00:50.249
&gt;&gt; To make this decision concrete, let's
consider a business purchasing decision

00:00:50.249 --> 00:00:52.953
most of us make,
buying stock in a public company.

00:00:52.953 --> 00:00:56.270
How do you put together
a good stock portfolio?

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:59.990
Which stocks are good buys and
which ones are not?

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:03.402
&gt;&gt; So as usual, last week, I was
talking to one of my golf buddies and

00:01:03.402 --> 00:01:06.570
he was telling me what
a great stock picker he is.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:10.680
He's never ever told me about any
bad investments he's made but

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.345
he always tells me about
the great investments he makes.

00:01:14.345 --> 00:01:19.215
The thing about this guy is his
investment strategy is incredibly simple.

00:01:19.215 --> 00:01:23.375
He tries out products, if he likes
the products, he buys the stock.

00:01:23.375 --> 00:01:25.515
And the question is, does that make sense?

00:01:25.515 --> 00:01:31.015
Is his investment strategy a strategy
that's going to make you a lot of money?

00:01:31.015 --> 00:01:32.071
&gt;&gt; Well, Jonathan,

00:01:32.071 --> 00:01:36.302
your buddy's argument sounds pretty
convincing if we hold all else equal.

00:01:36.302 --> 00:01:40.450
All else equal, a firm that makes good
products is certainly a better investment

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.348
than a firm that makes bad products.

00:01:42.348 --> 00:01:44.151
But can we hold all else equal?

00:01:44.151 --> 00:01:48.197
And as we've now seen several times,
the answer to that question is usually no.

00:01:48.197 --> 00:01:50.072
We have to think this through carefully.

00:01:50.072 --> 00:01:53.730
&gt;&gt; And the thing that is holding all
else equal is he's not worrying about

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:54.985
the price.

00:01:54.985 --> 00:01:59.742
So holding all else equal if I have two
investments and they cost the same,

00:01:59.742 --> 00:02:04.750
I would much rather invest for
the good company than a bad company.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:08.570
But as we discussed last time,
you can never hold all else equal.

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:12.780
If the company is a particularly good
company, it's going to have a high price.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:17.824
So really what you're interested in
is not whether the company is good or

00:02:17.824 --> 00:02:23.048
bad but whether or not the price of the
company is a good price or a bad price.

00:02:23.048 --> 00:02:26.784
&gt;&gt; Yes, because overpaying for a good
company is a bad investment strategy,

00:02:26.784 --> 00:02:31.280
whereas underpaying for a bad company will
make you a lot of money going forward.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.764
So it's not just about
what you're getting, but

00:02:33.764 --> 00:02:35.428
also what you're paying for it.

00:02:35.428 --> 00:02:39.041
&gt;&gt; And so the real question is not
whether the company is good or bad, but

00:02:39.041 --> 00:02:41.360
what price you're paying for the company?

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:47.161
And I'd like to make the argument that for
non professional, it's going to be very

00:02:47.161 --> 00:02:52.170
rare or next to impossible to find
investments whose prices are attractive.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:55.945
Or another way of saying this,
is it's going to be really hard for

00:02:55.945 --> 00:02:59.690
such a person to find good
deals in the stock market.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:03.176
&gt;&gt; What it's all about is about
competition and whether or not you have

00:03:03.176 --> 00:03:07.105
a competitive advantage that you can
exploit when you're picking stocks.

00:03:07.105 --> 00:03:10.927
Because if there's a good deal but
everybody already knows about it,

00:03:10.927 --> 00:03:15.400
then everybody will want to invest in
it and everybody will put in by orders.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.380
There going to be too many buyers and
not enough sellers.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.923
And so the price will go up and
the investment opportunity will disappear.

00:03:21.923 --> 00:03:26.483
And so the only way in which you can
make money investing in good companies

00:03:26.483 --> 00:03:30.296
between quotation marks is if
you arrive at the party early,

00:03:30.296 --> 00:03:35.082
meaning you find out that the products
that the company makes are better than

00:03:35.082 --> 00:03:37.735
what people thought up until that point.

00:03:37.735 --> 00:03:41.331
And so if you're the first one to figure
that out and you get into that stock

00:03:41.331 --> 00:03:45.410
early, there may still be a possibility
for you to make some extra returns.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:49.000
But if you're arriving at the party at
exactly the same time as everybody else,

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.380
then obviously,
you're not going to be making extra money.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:55.062
&gt;&gt; And it's not easy to
arrive at that party early.

00:03:55.062 --> 00:03:56.295
And the reason is,

00:03:56.295 --> 00:04:01.700
there are a lot of people who devote their
entire lives to looking for good parties.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:07.540
And it's unlikely that individually
investors will have the time or

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:12.256
the resources to do a better job than
the people whose career is built

00:04:12.256 --> 00:04:15.000
on trying to arrive at the party early.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.280
It's like a sports fan stepping
onto a football field and

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:23.808
thinking they can out play Tom Brady.

00:04:23.808 --> 00:04:26.012
It's just not a very likely event.

00:04:26.012 --> 00:04:29.050
&gt;&gt; Indeed, and so I mean,
it's not that there are no good and

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:31.223
bad investment decisions in the market.

00:04:31.223 --> 00:04:35.959
I think that the main thing is,
can average investors tell them apart?

00:04:35.959 --> 00:04:37.757
Because to be able to tell them apart,

00:04:37.757 --> 00:04:40.526
you need to have something
that other people don't have.

00:04:40.526 --> 00:04:44.382
That's exactly that competitive advantage
point and, of course, by definition,

00:04:44.382 --> 00:04:47.650
the average investor doesn't have
a competitive advantage compared to

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.275
everybody else.

00:04:49.275 --> 00:04:52.511
&gt;&gt; Jules,
when I teach this concept in class,

00:04:52.511 --> 00:04:57.945
I always tell an old joke which finance
professors have told for decades.

00:04:57.945 --> 00:04:59.425
The joke goes like this.

00:04:59.425 --> 00:05:03.506
A finance professor and his student
are walking down the street, and

00:05:03.506 --> 00:05:06.120
they see $100 bill lying on the ground.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:09.816
And then the student reaches down
to pick the $100 bill up, and

00:05:09.816 --> 00:05:12.991
the professor says to the student,
don't bother.

00:05:12.991 --> 00:05:16.780
If that really was $100 bill,
it will be taken already.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:20.252
And then I add to this joke an addendum,
and

00:05:20.252 --> 00:05:25.170
the addendum I add is,
I look at my students and I say to them,

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:30.491
how many of you have actually
found a $100 bill on the ground?

00:05:30.491 --> 00:05:31.837
And nobody raised their hand.

00:05:31.837 --> 00:05:34.490
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Nobody raises their hand.

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:39.468
And the ensuing silence is exactly
the point, which is A, if there is $100

00:05:39.468 --> 00:05:44.297
bill that's lying on the ground,
it's going to be picked up very quickly so

00:05:44.297 --> 00:05:47.177
the likelihood of you
finding it is very low.

00:05:47.177 --> 00:05:51.920
And B, if you have $100 bill in your
pocket, you're going to look after

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:56.140
it very carefully and so
you're very unlikely to drop it.

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:59.243
&gt;&gt; So, well, one thing that you
can add to that though, and

00:05:59.243 --> 00:06:03.449
that that really introduces that idea of
competition into this joke is that it

00:06:03.449 --> 00:06:06.010
depends a bit on how busy the street is.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:08.514
If the street is completely
full of people, that of

00:06:08.514 --> 00:06:12.900
course the probability that that $100 bill
will stay lying there is incredibly low.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.422
If the street is abandoned and
nobody's there,

00:06:15.422 --> 00:06:18.816
that of course the $100 bill
might lie there for a bit longer.

00:06:18.816 --> 00:06:23.136
Although of course, once it would become
known that $100 bills are lying around on

00:06:23.136 --> 00:06:27.299
the street all the time, my prediction is
that we'll get really busy really soon.

00:06:27.299 --> 00:06:30.410
&gt;&gt; And also, your competitive advantage.

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:36.624
If you happen to live on a street that's
a very quiet street and somebody dropped

00:06:36.624 --> 00:06:43.210
$100 bill, your likelihood of picking it
up is much higher than an average person.

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:44.200
&gt;&gt; Yes, absolutely.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:48.401
Another addendum to this example,
which I really liked to make in class is

00:06:48.401 --> 00:06:52.145
this idea that, suppose that
there's this building out there.

00:06:52.145 --> 00:06:56.006
And you are convinced that it's
full of $100 bills and actually,

00:06:56.006 --> 00:06:58.070
you talk to a lot of people.

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:01.465
And a lot of people say there are $100
bills lying in this building all

00:07:01.465 --> 00:07:05.361
over the place and you think to yourself,
I should be going to this building because

00:07:05.361 --> 00:07:09.070
why on earth is nobody going there
to pick up all these $100 bills?

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:12.691
You get so overconfident, you go into the
building, start picking up the one hundred

00:07:12.691 --> 00:07:15.170
dollar bills,
the building collapses and you're dead.

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:18.887
And so in the end, there was a reason
why people were actually not going into

00:07:18.887 --> 00:07:20.783
that building to pick up those bills.

00:07:20.783 --> 00:07:24.568
It was just that you are not realizing
what the rest of the market knew that you

00:07:24.568 --> 00:07:25.347
didn't know.

00:07:25.347 --> 00:07:28.855
And you should have thought it
through better and think why is it?

00:07:28.855 --> 00:07:33.076
Can I understand why the rest of the
market is not going into that building to

00:07:33.076 --> 00:07:35.400
pick up those $100 bills?

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:38.487
&gt;&gt; It's another all else equal mistake,
right?

00:07:38.487 --> 00:07:43.024
If I know there's a really good deal
out there, then all else equal,

00:07:43.024 --> 00:07:45.460
I should take advantage of the deal.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:47.760
But everything is not all else equal.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:52.711
The fact that nobody else is taking
advantage of the deal tells me that there

00:07:52.711 --> 00:07:55.350
must be something else going on.

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.340
It's too easy.

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:02.430
&gt;&gt; Indeed, so, Johnson, how does
information get into prices, right?

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:06.316
So suppose that The price of a stock
is lower than it should be and

00:08:06.316 --> 00:08:08.920
therefore it is a good buying opportunity.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:13.656
So I just said it was a given quality for
a firm that the price of the stock is not

00:08:13.656 --> 00:08:17.134
reflecting that call that
means the price is too low, so

00:08:17.134 --> 00:08:19.502
how is that price going to take quality?

00:08:19.502 --> 00:08:23.167
&gt;&gt; Well I mean, first of all,
everybody is going to want that deal.

00:08:23.167 --> 00:08:25.340
Everybody wants a good deal.

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:28.220
So, people are going to
start buying that stock.

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:32.560
At the same time somebody
has to sell that stock.

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:35.450
If it is a good deal to buy,
it is a bad deal to sell.

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:40.650
So that means not only am I getting a good
deal, somebody else is getting a bad deal.

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:44.140
And I would argue that that's
not going to last very long.

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:48.094
They're very quickly the people selling
are going to say wait a minute I don't

00:08:48.094 --> 00:08:49.903
want to sell it for such a low price.

00:08:49.903 --> 00:08:53.871
And the people buying are going to
queue up to buy at that low price and

00:08:53.871 --> 00:08:56.384
very quickly the price will start rising.

00:08:56.384 --> 00:09:01.136
And will rising until the point
where it's no longer a good deal.

00:09:01.136 --> 00:09:03.791
In deed, and the same thing
of course holds when we tick

00:09:03.791 --> 00:09:06.159
the other scenario where
the price is too high and

00:09:06.159 --> 00:09:09.250
then the price will be bid
down through the same process.

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:12.640
To arrive at the right level of the price.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:17.079
And really the only way you can get
a good deal in market is you have to

00:09:17.079 --> 00:09:19.306
be the first person who arrives.

00:09:19.306 --> 00:09:23.986
And you don't have to buy and you buy
a limited amount and then you can make

00:09:23.986 --> 00:09:28.820
money, and there are people that do that
but they need a lot of resources and

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:33.890
a lot of skill to find those special
deals that nobody else sees.

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:38.346
So the key thing then is to determine
whether or not the price is higher or

00:09:38.346 --> 00:09:41.100
lower than where it should be.

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:44.440
And so the question then is,
how do you measure the should be?

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:49.135
And so therefore often what we talk about
is what's called price multiples which

00:09:49.135 --> 00:09:51.355
are relative measures of valuation.

00:09:51.355 --> 00:09:55.580
And so for different firms she can take
the ratio of the price per share to

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:57.918
the earnings per share of the company.

00:09:57.918 --> 00:10:01.337
Or you can take the ratio of the price
per share of the company to what's called

00:10:01.337 --> 00:10:02.504
the book value of equity.

00:10:02.504 --> 00:10:06.164
Which is a measure per share of the
replacement value of the plant property

00:10:06.164 --> 00:10:09.836
equipment and all the capital that
the firm uses to produce its products.

00:10:09.836 --> 00:10:14.260
And some savvy investors understand
the all else equal argument and

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:17.993
understand that it's
the relative price that matters.

00:10:17.993 --> 00:10:22.612
They almost always just talk in terms
of multiples rather than in price.

00:10:22.612 --> 00:10:26.977
So let's go and talk to Cliff Asness
because Cliff Asness is an expert in

00:10:26.977 --> 00:10:32.780
finding good deals and markets and he has
a particular approach to go about this.

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:34.370
He's been very successful at it.

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:36.607
So let's see what he
has to say about this.

00:10:36.607 --> 00:10:39.900
&gt;&gt; Okay, well, welcome to the show.

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:41.710
We're very honored to have you.

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:46.287
For people who don't know Cliff Asness
is manager and co founder of

00:10:46.287 --> 00:10:51.204
AQR Capital Management one of
the largest asset managers in the world.

00:10:51.204 --> 00:10:56.529
And I would say not only that universally
regarded as one of the most successful

00:10:56.529 --> 00:11:01.705
asset managers in modern history and
it's really an honor to have you here.

00:11:01.705 --> 00:11:04.742
&gt;&gt; Thank you Jonathan I think
that's a gross exaggeration but

00:11:04.742 --> 00:11:06.390
I enjoy gross exaggeration so.

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:09.448
&gt;&gt; Welcome we're very happy to have you.

00:11:09.448 --> 00:11:14.275
&gt;&gt; Certainly our speakers the objective
in this particular episode is to

00:11:14.275 --> 00:11:18.792
talk about what has commonly
become known as efficient markets.

00:11:18.792 --> 00:11:21.890
The idea that a good
stock is not a good buy.

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:24.130
It's a cheap stock, that's a good buy.

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:27.074
So with that in mind,
I have two questions for you,

00:11:27.074 --> 00:11:29.960
one question is a very general question.

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:32.000
How hard is it to find
good deals in the market?

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.481
But I'm going to make that very specific,

00:11:33.481 --> 00:11:36.780
because this morning large brush my teeth
and I was thinking about this interview.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:39.200
I was thinking about
that very specifically.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:43.360
Lots and lots of architects go and
try to do what you do.

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:48.460
And the thing is, and we know academics
are very smart, but the thing is,

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:49.830
not all of them succeed.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:56.620
What is it, that allows some people to
succeed and other people not to succeed?

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:58.562
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all,
it's nice to know in the era of Zoom,

00:11:58.562 --> 00:12:00.450
you're still brushing your teeth.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:03.590
I'm not sure all of us are doing that.

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:05.900
I'm going to give you
kind of a weird answer.

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:11.265
Obviously, some people can translate the
theoretical to the practical better and

00:12:11.265 --> 00:12:14.789
the real world trading costs and
size you can run and and

00:12:14.789 --> 00:12:20.840
judgments about whether something is real
or whether it's a data mined artifact.

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:22.117
Those all do very but

00:12:22.117 --> 00:12:26.560
I'm going to give you an answer that
maybe would be a little surprising.

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:33.248
The capacity to withstand pain and
the capacity to explain

00:12:33.248 --> 00:12:38.810
when things are not working,
why they're not working.

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:41.689
You have to be good at
what you do long term.

00:12:41.689 --> 00:12:46.648
If you could stick with it and convince
others to stick with it I think you'd at

00:12:46.648 --> 00:12:49.942
least have a reasonably
good investment product.

00:12:49.942 --> 00:12:54.328
Those two things that are often
an afterthought, you being able to

00:12:54.328 --> 00:12:59.117
tolerate it and don't think you're
not your worst client on occasion.

00:12:59.117 --> 00:13:04.249
And being able explain it to others,
in a real world investing strategies

00:13:04.249 --> 00:13:08.740
even if you're very good,
work a little more than they don't.

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:11.770
They don't work all the time.

00:13:11.770 --> 00:13:15.812
People talk about stocks for the long run.

00:13:15.812 --> 00:13:17.230
I'm in geek speak.

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:19.910
And I'm sure your students
are familiar with these things.

00:13:19.910 --> 00:13:22.940
The stock market's like a point for
Sharpe ratio.

00:13:22.940 --> 00:13:24.480
Go simulate that in Excel.

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:29.574
It's excruciatingly hard
to stick with at times.

00:13:29.574 --> 00:13:33.646
And if anything, my answer to that
question is probably pretty different than

00:13:33.646 --> 00:13:35.445
it would have been 20 years ago.

00:13:35.445 --> 00:13:38.640
I think I appreciate that side
of it both for ourselves and for

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:41.396
clients how tough it is to
stick with a strategy, for

00:13:41.396 --> 00:13:45.803
truly everyone talks about the long term
actually being long term is a superpower.

00:13:45.803 --> 00:13:49.175
And in investing if you can
do it it is very hard to do.

00:13:49.175 --> 00:13:54.212
&gt;&gt; That's a very surprising answer Cliff
because we spend a lot of time also in our

00:13:54.212 --> 00:13:59.267
classroom and in our research thinking
about how to best design the strategy.

00:13:59.267 --> 00:14:03.904
But what you're saying is that what is
really an important skill is to convince

00:14:03.904 --> 00:14:08.275
both yourself as well as your clients
to stick with the ideas that you had.

00:14:08.275 --> 00:14:12.200
And so the ability to withstand adversity
apparently is the most important skill.

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:14.565
Now we know that that's
generally true in life, but

00:14:14.565 --> 00:14:18.060
apparently that is true here in
the investment world as well.

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:20.710
&gt;&gt; So tell when you're on the golf course.

00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:24.701
You always got your body telling you
how much money he's made in the stock

00:14:24.701 --> 00:14:26.120
market, right?

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:27.960
Let's assume they're telling the truth.

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:31.363
How do they do this without
the resources of AQR and

00:14:31.363 --> 00:14:37.440
you're in all the intelligence of all
the employees you have and all of that?

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:41.370
&gt;&gt; I think when it comes to people who
are talking to their friends on the golf

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:45.610
course, I don't golf so
I'm trying to come up with someplace else.

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:48.229
I do go to cocktail parties,
occasionally back [INAUDIBLE] parties.

00:14:48.229 --> 00:14:51.430
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You get a lot of people talking about

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:52.880
the stock or even the market.

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:56.183
I don't think the market
is very easy to time and

00:14:56.183 --> 00:14:58.837
you hear a lot of opinions about that.

00:14:58.837 --> 00:15:00.752
Jonathan you said something important.

00:15:00.752 --> 00:15:04.147
You said let's assume what
they're telling you it's true,

00:15:04.147 --> 00:15:07.944
a I don't always assume that but for
the sake of your argument I will.

00:15:07.944 --> 00:15:12.509
But what I won't assume is that
the people who lose terribly are as

00:15:12.509 --> 00:15:15.588
anxious to tell their golf course stories.

00:15:15.588 --> 00:15:17.107
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you're so right about that,

00:15:17.107 --> 00:15:19.952
people rarely tell me how poorly
they've done on their stock picks.

00:15:19.952 --> 00:15:23.111
I mean, this is also one of
the reasons for why we study,

00:15:23.111 --> 00:15:27.347
when we study investment managers,
that when returns are self reported,

00:15:27.347 --> 00:15:30.307
of course the funds that did
well are much more likely

00:15:30.307 --> 00:15:33.762
to report the results than
the funds that didn't do so well.

00:15:33.762 --> 00:15:39.764
&gt;&gt; So by the way this is not different
than fear of missing out FOMO on Facebook.

00:15:39.764 --> 00:15:43.186
They tell us as a epidemic of depressed
people because everyone thinks

00:15:43.186 --> 00:15:46.340
that other people's lives
are better than theirs.

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:49.999
because they're looking at their
Facebook or their Instagram and

00:15:49.999 --> 00:15:51.765
all the fun they're having.

00:15:51.765 --> 00:15:56.346
You don't hear about people's problems
on Facebook as exceptions to that of

00:15:56.346 --> 00:15:59.770
course but
as a rule they're positive posts.

00:15:59.770 --> 00:16:05.170
You don't see people at cocktail parties
going, man, I suck at stock picking.

00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:09.490
Let me tell you about the, I made all
this money but I lost it all on these.

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:12.860
Again, there are exceptions.

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:16.500
I would listen to that person at
the cocktail party, they'd be kind of fun.

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:20.200
The honest person who's telling you that,
but that's not the rule.

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:24.710
So you do have to be very careful
of the financial version of FOMO.

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:28.252
Where your people are cherry
picking their winners,

00:16:28.252 --> 00:16:34.110
even if they're not lying about them and
who speaks up is being cherry picked.

00:16:34.110 --> 00:16:39.050
So I will call myself a cynic that
the individual doing this casually

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:42.082
can add a whole lot of value, certainly,

00:16:42.082 --> 00:16:46.630
at the concentrated picking
of individual security level.

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:50.822
But I made a big concession to my
efficient market roots that I think there

00:16:50.822 --> 00:16:53.260
are some people who clearly can.

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:57.538
&gt;&gt; When you think about we all have a golf
buddy or somebody like a golf buddy,

00:16:57.538 --> 00:17:00.230
telling us what a genius they are.

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:05.692
One of the things that you notice
when people tell you about their

00:17:05.692 --> 00:17:11.562
investment strategy in a social
environment is more often than not,

00:17:11.562 --> 00:17:16.642
the investment strategy is so
simple, it is so clear to do.

00:17:16.642 --> 00:17:21.598
And I always think to myself,
when these people tell me the strategy,

00:17:21.598 --> 00:17:27.060
what makes them think that nobody else
has thought of such a simple strategy?

00:17:28.100 --> 00:17:31.740
&gt;&gt; And also the question of, who is on
the other side of that trade, right?

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:36.066
I mean, this famous saying,
if you don't know who the sucker is,

00:17:36.066 --> 00:17:37.390
it is probably you.

00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:37.996
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:37.996 --> 00:17:41.092
&gt;&gt; Jonathan,
I think you're thinking the same way.

00:17:41.092 --> 00:17:45.640
But I would sum it up as they
leave price out of their strategy.

00:17:45.640 --> 00:17:51.159
Often, their strategy is about what
they think is going to be a big thing.

00:17:51.159 --> 00:17:56.401
Back in 99-2000 during the first
wave of kind of tech stock mania,

00:17:56.401 --> 00:18:02.078
people would say things like, well,
the Internet's going to change the world.

00:18:02.078 --> 00:18:07.699
And I think that was fairly
obviously true at the time.

00:18:07.699 --> 00:18:09.633
The notion, all right,

00:18:09.633 --> 00:18:14.341
should you be paying an infinite
multiple for drkoop.com?

00:18:14.341 --> 00:18:15.432
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:18:15.432 --> 00:18:18.377
&gt;&gt; He was the Surgeon General,
who then launched the.com at the time.

00:18:18.377 --> 00:18:20.440
There were many other examples.

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:23.960
The NASDAQ as a whole,
NASDAQ 100 was selling for

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:26.930
about 100 price to earnings multiples.

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:32.778
So the question, aside from trading,
trading is a whole different world.

00:18:32.778 --> 00:18:37.105
If you're trying to predict what happens
tomorrow or even the next few months or

00:18:37.105 --> 00:18:40.110
even even the next year, that's a lot.

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:43.840
Warren Buffett always talks about
a voting versus a weighing machine.

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:45.270
That is a voting machine.

00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:47.854
If other people decide to
jump on the bandwagon,

00:18:47.854 --> 00:18:52.040
a good trader doesn't necessarily
need to have a big grasp of price.

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:53.260
I'm a little cynical.

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:56.840
There are great natural traders who
can just feel the market also, but

00:18:56.840 --> 00:18:59.110
I don't dismiss the possibility.

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:02.046
But if you're going to hold
something as an investor for

00:19:02.046 --> 00:19:06.980
a decent length of time, there are really
basically two things that go into it.

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:11.528
The quality and can be multi dimensional
quality of how well they execute,

00:19:11.528 --> 00:19:14.848
how fast they can grow,
what markets they're in, and

00:19:14.848 --> 00:19:18.060
what price you're paying to begin with.

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:19.530
Let me tell you a story.

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:23.026
This is from the tech bubble, 99-2000.

00:19:23.026 --> 00:19:27.063
I remember speaking to an audience and
the audience was very cynical.

00:19:27.063 --> 00:19:31.575
I'm doing my value has never looked
more ridiculously stretched and

00:19:31.575 --> 00:19:35.709
there are people there somebody
raised their favorite stock.

00:19:35.709 --> 00:19:39.655
I forget which one it was but I've said
it was Cisco, the networking stock for

00:19:39.655 --> 00:19:43.310
at least ten years, so I'm going to
stick with that though it may be a lie.

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:49.571
And I asked them, all right, basically,
this question, if you're going to

00:19:49.571 --> 00:19:54.594
have to hold Cisco not for a month but
for 10, 20 years, you love it.

00:19:54.594 --> 00:19:59.660
I get it, you love it and we disagree,
at what price would you get out?

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:06.710
And I was trying to induce them to answer
the question that we're groping around.

00:20:06.710 --> 00:20:08.970
And the answer, I still love the answer.

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:09.797
Thank God,

00:20:09.797 --> 00:20:14.352
I don't remember who it was because
it would be very mean of me if I did.

00:20:14.352 --> 00:20:18.246
But the answer the person
gave me at the event was,

00:20:18.246 --> 00:20:23.558
if it was selling for X, well,
if it dropped to half x, I'd be out.

00:20:23.558 --> 00:20:27.563
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; They want the opposite way.

00:20:27.563 --> 00:20:30.547
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And perfect.

00:20:30.547 --> 00:20:32.827
&gt;&gt; Again, they might have misunderstood.

00:20:32.827 --> 00:20:35.823
I really did try to preface it
with this as a long term hold.

00:20:35.823 --> 00:20:40.515
So basically saying, if I could buy this
for the next 20 years at half the price,

00:20:40.515 --> 00:20:43.849
they were giving me a momentum
answer to a value question.

00:20:43.849 --> 00:20:46.300
&gt;&gt; What I like about that analogy is,

00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:51.878
it not only illustrates that professionals
can make all else equal mistakes,

00:20:51.878 --> 00:20:57.050
but it's another example of where it
is difficult to stick by your guns.

00:20:58.120 --> 00:21:01.964
That the earlier point you make that
a part of the skill of a professional is

00:21:01.964 --> 00:21:04.634
somebody is willing to
stick with their strategy.

00:21:04.634 --> 00:21:07.939
&gt;&gt; So earlier, you made a remark which
I found very interesting which was in

00:21:07.939 --> 00:21:11.678
the golf course the person was willing to
discuss their mistakes would actually be

00:21:11.678 --> 00:21:13.918
somebody that you would
be willing to listen to.

00:21:13.918 --> 00:21:19.660
And you also seem very willing and open to
discuss the things that have gone poorly.

00:21:19.660 --> 00:21:22.308
Why do you think this is so
difficult for most people to do?

00:21:22.308 --> 00:21:23.328
&gt;&gt; Well, for one thing,

00:21:23.328 --> 00:21:26.300
I just think most people haven't
thought through the strategy.

00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:30.280
I think the world respects,
I'll be honest.

00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:31.990
It's self-serving of me.

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:34.199
I think being open and
honest about these things.

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:39.468
The world respects it, not that I'm
always respected, don't always work.

00:21:39.468 --> 00:21:44.538
[LAUGH] It doesn't work each time and
don't work with each person, but I think

00:21:44.538 --> 00:21:50.010
on average, people do sense who's bs-ing
them and who's giving them the truth.

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:54.760
I do think I have gotten
better at it over my career.

00:21:54.760 --> 00:21:58.610
It's kind of like all
the people do sports analytics.

00:21:58.610 --> 00:22:01.745
Talk about how coaches who've been
successful over the long term

00:22:01.745 --> 00:22:03.235
are actually better coaches.

00:22:03.235 --> 00:22:06.385
Not because they've learned
what play works, but

00:22:06.385 --> 00:22:11.314
because they're more steadfast in
doing the play they believe will work

00:22:11.314 --> 00:22:15.294
even if it's going to get them yelled at,
if it doesn't.

00:22:15.294 --> 00:22:17.690
So you do improve upon this.

00:22:17.690 --> 00:22:22.468
But I think most people, they don't quite
get that if they leave with all the things

00:22:22.468 --> 00:22:25.617
they've done wrong,
they can still impress people.

00:22:25.617 --> 00:22:30.930
They can still have people think
they know what they're doing.

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:32.170
And this doesn't work for everyone.

00:22:32.170 --> 00:22:35.533
If you're genuinely bad at what you're
doing and you're a bit of a boob,

00:22:35.533 --> 00:22:36.890
leading with seven errors and

00:22:36.890 --> 00:22:41.040
then never following it up with
substance is really not a good strategy.

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:42.573
So it does come down and

00:22:42.573 --> 00:22:47.666
maybe I'm over endowed with this with
a certain level of self confidence.

00:22:47.666 --> 00:22:53.112
Admitting your mistakes requires
self confidence because you have

00:22:53.112 --> 00:22:59.330
to have faith that people will
make a judgment on the totality.

00:22:59.330 --> 00:23:04.020
Not on the few things you told
them that you were stupid about.

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:10.663
So if I gave a life lesson, I've talked
to my kids about this, that is one.

00:23:10.663 --> 00:23:15.094
I don't have a lot of great life lessons,
but that is certainly one.

00:23:15.094 --> 00:23:17.621
That if you're down
the middle about yourself and

00:23:17.621 --> 00:23:19.553
no one's perfectly down the middle.

00:23:19.553 --> 00:23:21.449
We're all a little biased our own story.

00:23:21.449 --> 00:23:25.510
But if you're down in the middle as you
can be, have some faith in other people.

00:23:26.580 --> 00:23:30.625
I think it would work out for
many more people better than they think,

00:23:30.625 --> 00:23:31.409
not just me.

00:23:31.409 --> 00:23:33.525
I might have lucked into
the strategy over time.

00:23:33.525 --> 00:23:34.124
&gt;&gt; I love that.

00:23:34.124 --> 00:23:36.406
&gt;&gt; I think actually,
there's an extension to this,

00:23:36.406 --> 00:23:38.580
which is not only do you
admit your mistakes, but

00:23:38.580 --> 00:23:41.936
you learn from your mistakes,
I genuinely mean learn from your mistakes.

00:23:41.936 --> 00:23:45.890
I don't mean, I made a mistake and
I repeat the same mistake again.

00:23:45.890 --> 00:23:50.480
I really think to myself,
what caused me to make that mistake and

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:53.110
how could I not do that again?

00:23:53.110 --> 00:23:57.590
I think it's a very difficult thing
to do and I think human beings really

00:23:57.590 --> 00:24:00.586
I struggle with it,
I think the sippy future podcast for

00:24:00.586 --> 00:24:04.540
business decision makers,
this is something we should focus on.

00:24:04.540 --> 00:24:09.790
Why is it so hard to admit that
you've made a mistake and act on it?

00:24:09.790 --> 00:24:11.110
&gt;&gt; Of course you're exactly right.

00:24:11.110 --> 00:24:14.570
That is the second half,
admitting you made a mistake is lovely.

00:24:14.570 --> 00:24:18.650
And it's it makes for
a very cathartic conversation.

00:24:18.650 --> 00:24:23.620
But if you learn zero from that mistake,
admitting it doesn't actually help.

00:24:23.620 --> 00:24:26.610
The second part is pretty important too.

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:29.450
So you're right I think they
often go hand in glove.

00:24:29.450 --> 00:24:34.016
I think that person and not just me by any
means, but the person who can admit to

00:24:34.016 --> 00:24:38.581
a mistake, almost by definition is at
least halfway down the road to asking,

00:24:38.581 --> 00:24:42.190
why they made a mistake and
why they might not going forward?

00:24:42.190 --> 00:24:45.870
I mean, it's kind of odd to imagine
someone admitting to a litany of mistakes,

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:49.382
and then saying, well, what are you
going to do to prevent that going forward

00:24:49.382 --> 00:24:52.226
and have them, if you have this
conversation with someone,

00:24:52.226 --> 00:24:55.640
they've made a couple of errors
even the life's been good.

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:58.395
And you go, well, what do you learn
from those errors, and they go, nothing.

00:24:58.395 --> 00:25:01.000
[LAUGH] That will be odd.

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:03.779
I'm talking about ex ante errors,
not ex post errors, and

00:25:03.779 --> 00:25:06.180
that's another one of the huge problems,
right?

00:25:07.180 --> 00:25:10.190
I think ex ante,
we launched too aggressively.

00:25:10.190 --> 00:25:13.634
In 1998, at AQR ex post,
we got one of the most

00:25:13.634 --> 00:25:18.230
savage value drawdowns again
the next three years was wonderful.

00:25:18.230 --> 00:25:24.120
So I'll always throw that in the marketing
at Has to go on, but that was ex post.

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:27.184
But I do think we made an ex ante mistake,

00:25:27.184 --> 00:25:31.420
there is also if I can be
a little crass about it.

00:25:31.420 --> 00:25:33.608
And someone at AQR is
going to yell at me for this.

00:25:33.608 --> 00:25:39.112
Our legal world has people terrified to
say the words I made a mistake ever and

00:25:39.112 --> 00:25:43.412
they're doing their jobs and
they're wonderful people,

00:25:43.412 --> 00:25:49.930
at least the ones that are firm I actually
like our legal and compliance people.

00:25:49.930 --> 00:25:53.594
But they never,
I scare them when I say things yeah,

00:25:53.594 --> 00:25:55.690
I think we got that one wrong.

00:25:56.810 --> 00:26:00.910
And they're, I can't say that I'm, does
anyone think we get everything, right?

00:26:00.910 --> 00:26:05.484
Is there anyone on earth who thinks that,
my mother didn't think that, and

00:26:05.484 --> 00:26:07.770
she was biassed towards me.

00:26:07.770 --> 00:26:13.620
So I think that part of our culture, and
it doesn't mean it's a net negative.

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:17.957
There are a lot of legal protections we
have, and it does protect the world from

00:26:17.957 --> 00:26:22.920
some things, but that part of the culture
also pushes any corporate Executive.

00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:27.330
Away from the words yeah we were
wrong about that, one area bad.

00:26:27.330 --> 00:26:30.886
&gt;&gt; So yeah, one of the reasons why we
decided to do the podcast to begin

00:26:30.886 --> 00:26:33.680
with is because people make
mistakes all the time and

00:26:33.680 --> 00:26:35.856
it is very difficult to learn from them.

00:26:35.856 --> 00:26:38.355
And so
you really need to talk them through, and

00:26:38.355 --> 00:26:42.292
see the commonalities in the mistakes
that people are making so thank you so

00:26:42.292 --> 00:26:45.917
much Cliff for spending the time with us,
it's been really great and

00:26:45.917 --> 00:26:48.426
it's been a lot of fun to
&gt;&gt; Now it has been fun thank

00:26:48.426 --> 00:26:49.880
you guys Guys.

00:26:49.880 --> 00:26:52.855
&gt;&gt; Thanks everybody for listening
we hope you enjoyed the podcast and

00:26:52.855 --> 00:26:55.800
the interview with Cliff Asness
we learned a lot from him.

00:26:55.800 --> 00:27:00.053
Certainly what he explained was that
not putting price into the equation

00:27:00.053 --> 00:27:04.385
is going to make you make a mistake I
think that came through very clearly.

00:27:04.385 --> 00:27:08.615
But I was surprised with the fact that the
most important investment management skill

00:27:08.615 --> 00:27:10.799
was to stick with it and
to stick through it.

00:27:10.799 --> 00:27:15.424
I hadn't expected that to be the answer to
the question what is the skill in shortest

00:27:15.424 --> 00:27:15.960
supply.

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:19.889
So now next time we're going to
apply the same all else equal logic

00:27:19.889 --> 00:27:21.253
to money management.

00:27:21.253 --> 00:27:24.981
So, should you put your money
with the best manager and

00:27:24.981 --> 00:27:29.470
best managers say a five star
rating manager by Morningstar?

00:27:29.470 --> 00:27:31.020
If you put your money with that manager,

00:27:31.020 --> 00:27:33.310
are you going to get high returns
going forward, yes or no?

00:27:33.310 --> 00:27:35.510
That is what we are exploring next time.

00:27:35.510 --> 00:27:38.651
And the guest we have for
that is Pete Berger, who is CEO of

00:27:38.651 --> 00:27:44.170
the Fortress Investment Group, one of
the largest global investment managers.

00:27:44.170 --> 00:27:47.280
&gt;&gt; Thanks for
listening to all else equal podcast.

